Prometheus77 wrote:
>
> A couple of tips when playing as a knight:
> * Don't get burdened. You can #jump and use your lance to get away
> from/kill just about anything, but not if you're burdened. Drop stuff
> you don't immediately need (especially heavy things like spellbooks &
> potions) by the stairs

This is valid advice for all early game characters.  The difference is
knights start out closer to burdened than other characters so inventory
control is that much important.  In fact if you're finding yourself
burdened a lot while playing other character classes try playing knights
for a while.  Once you find the down stairs drop every item you don't
think is essential for that level in a local stash near the stairs.

> * Go to Minetown and back before going through the rest of the dungeon.
> Gnomes are easy kills and give you lots more experience than lichens
> and jackals, you can collect a good stash of daggers, and you'll get a
> guaranteed altar for BUC testing and an almost-guaranteed shop for
> price testing. You might even get lucky and find a magic lamp or bag of
> holding.

Notice there is advice to build up a quiver of daggers even for a class
who can never multifire.  That teaches much about the importance of
missile combat.  Even with single fire missile combat is so valuable
it's worth spending a skill slot on daggers.  Be sure to collect all
crossbow bolts and arrows to decide if you'll also eventually use a
crossbow or bow as an additional missile weapon.

> * When you're ready to #dip for Excalibur, here are some tips. Don't do
> it in Minetown, too risky that you'll tick off the guards. Some people
> wait until level 7, I never do. If you're worried about water
> moccasins/demons, a trick is to engrave Elbereth a couple of times
> around every open square next to the fountain and drop a gold piece on
> top before dipping. As soon as something hostile comes out, stand on
> one of those spots until the snakes or demon are far enough away that
> you can start #jumping to escape.

Knights start with a pony.  The pony grows to a horse then to a war
horse.  By the time you're down to mine town your horse will likely be
strong enough to take out the watchmen.  They won't supply an additional
long sword but they will supply plenty of armor items to be BUC checked
and tried on.  By the time you come back up from mines end your pet war
horse will be strong enough to take out the watch captain.  He will
supply either a long sword or a silver saber.  Once there is no
surviving watch go right ahead and dip for Excalibur in the mine town
fountains.  Especially if the watch captain supplies you with a spare
long sword so you don't have to worry about rust.  For that matter if
the watch captain supplies you with a silver saber that can be your off
hand wield for melee for the rest of the game.

> * It's easy to get burdened in Sokoban, also dangerous because of
> nasties like leocrottas and fire/soldier ants. Don't get burdened, just
> drop extra wands, potions, food rations etc. on the ground and put a
> boulder on top.

The levels of Sokoban are small enough that I suggest shuttling the
found items to near the down stairs.  I don't like wasting any boulder
on those levels.  One wrong move and you'll need any spare boulder.

> * If you're lucky enough to find a wand of fire or lightning, don't
> waste it on attacking bad guys. Use it to burn Elbereth into the
> ground, stand on the square, and kill off monsters as they run away.
> (As a knight, don't hit them after they've started fleeing or you'll
> lose an alignment point). Be careful though, a wand of lightning will
> blind you. But better blind and alive...

The single point of numerical alignment is so trivial I almost ignore
it.  I just try to count one hostile after each "Caitif" message.

> Good luck! After Valkyries, Knights are probably the best characters to play for beginners.

Valkyrie to learn the value of missile combat.  Knight to learn better
inventory management.


